  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "vjHdiFkTFGS2Ww5QpJFGHwok6dQ=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:39:02.769Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`RegistryClient` from `@powerhousedao/reactor-browser` is the client for a Powerhouse package registry, constructed with the registry's CDN base:\n\n```ts\nconst client = new RegistryClient(\"http://localhost:8080/-/cdn/\");\n```\n\nFour methods, and the interesting ones are the second and the fourth:\n\n- **`getPackages()`** \u2014 every package as `{ name, description, version, category, publisher, publisherUrl }`.\n- **`getPackagesByDocumentType(type)`** \u2014 the package names containing a given document type, e.g. `\"powerhouse/document-model\"`. This is the lookup behind \"you opened a document whose type nothing installed provides\": the registry can answer which package to install.\n- **`searchPackages(query)`** \u2014 **client-side** search across name and description, so it needs the package list first and no server-side search endpoint exists.\n- **`onPublish(cb)`** \u2014 subscribes to real-time publish notifications over **Server-Sent Events**, calling back with `{ packageName, version }` and returning an unsubscribe function.\n\nSSE rather than polling is the notable choice: a running Connect can learn that a package it depends on was republished without asking, which is what makes a runtime-installed package list feel live.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor-browser/src/registry/README.md` \u2014 whole file: constructor, getPackages, getPackagesByDocumentType, searchPackages, onPublish\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:39:02.769Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:39:02.769Z"
    }
  },
